<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Changing Forests – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Ecology Is Changing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Interpret non-verbal cues in communication. Communication: Facilitate peer discussions on various topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret classmates' non-verbal cues, like nodding or frowning, in discussions about environmental issues and adjust responses accordingly (e.g., uses a hand gesture to invite contribution and says, "I noticed you seemed interested; would you like to share your thoughts on recycling?").
Can facilitate discussions on ecological topics by prompting classmates to contribute ideas about conservation, using visuals like a deforestation map to spark conversation (e.g., "How can we reduce pollution in our area?").
Can interpret reactions, such as looking puzzled, during discussions on deforestation, using simpler language and drawings to explain (e.g., points to a simple diagram of a forest and says, "Deforestation means cutting down trees, which harms animals' habitats.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the mediation focus and connection to the "Forest Health Infographic" project.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various simple images related to "Our Changing Forests" (e.g., a healthy forest, a deforested area, recycling bins, polluted river). Students quickly identify the image and share one word describing their feeling or thought about it. The teacher notes down keywords, activating prior knowledge on the theme. (2-3 min)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to interpret non-verbal cues. Teacher asks a student about their infographic ideas and exaggerates a non-verbal cue (e.g., a puzzled look, a nod of understanding). Teacher says, "I noticed you seemed puzzled when I mentioned 'sustainable logging.' Does that term need more explanation?" The teacher then offers a simpler definition or drawing. This explicitly models interpreting cues and adapting explanations. (3-4 min)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a 'non-verbal cue'? Can you give an example?" (Expected: A gesture, facial expression. Example: Nodding.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to interpret these cues when someone is explaining something?" (Expected: To know if they understand, if they agree, to adjust our explanation.)</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input related to mediating information and the "Forest Health Infographic" project.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Changing Forests":</b> The teacher presents a short video clip (2-3 minutes) of two students discussing an infographic on climate change. One student is explaining the data, and the other is asking clarifying questions, showing varied non-verbal reactions (e.g., looking confused, nodding, shrugging). The teacher pauses the video at key moments to highlight how the explainer responds to these cues. The video features phrases like, "Deforestation has significantly affected biodiversity," and "If we preserve our forests, we will improve air quality." (6-7 min)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short educational video clip from an environmental organization explaining an infographic about ecological changes (e.g., "Impact of Deforestation in Latin America"). Genre: Informative explanation and peer dialogue. Register: Formal yet accessible. Format: Video presentation with pauses for discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting and re-communicating information, facilitating understanding, and responding to audience needs effectively, especially when discussing environmental impacts and conservation ideas.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify three non-verbal cues shown by the listener in the video and guess what they meant. (e.g., "A frown meant confusion.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify how the speaker adjusted their explanation based on these cues. (e.g., "The speaker used a simpler synonym for 'biodiversity' when the listener looked puzzled.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students list two comparative phrases they heard used to explain the infographic data (e.g., "Urban ecosystems have less biodiversity compared to rural areas.").</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation skills and target language for the "Forest Health Infographic" project.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpreting non-verbal cues, facilitating discussions, and simplifying complex concepts about environmental issues.
            <br><b>Grammar:</b> Present perfect, first and second conditionals, comparatives.
            <br><b>Vocabulary:</b> deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, ecosystems, conservation, air quality, forest health, impact, reduce, plant, improve, protect, healthier, significant.
            <br><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice /aɪ/ in 'life,' 'biodiversity,' and word stress in 'dramatic,' 'ecological.' Pragmatic: Asking about environmental impact, discussing conservation ideas. Sociolinguistic: Expressing concerns politely, using community-focused language.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Cue Cards Match (Controlled Practice)</b> (5 min)
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides students with "Non-Verbal Cue" cards (e.g., 'Frowning,' 'Nodding vigorously,' 'Looking away,' 'Hand on chin') and "Meaning" cards (e.g., 'Confusion,' 'Agreement/Understanding,' 'Disinterest/Distraction,' 'Deep thought/Questioning').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, students match the cue cards to their most likely meanings. Teacher circulates, checking for accurate interpretation and pronunciation of words like "diversity" /daɪˈvɜːrsɪti/.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Facilitation Role-Play (Semi-Controlled Practice)</b> (8 min)
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in groups of three. One student is the "Explainer" of a given environmental statement (e.g., "Deforestation has dramatically impacted local biodiversity."), another is the "Listener" who must intentionally display 2-3 specific non-verbal cues (e.g., a puzzled look, then a nod), and the third is the "Mediator."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Mediator's role is to interpret the Listener's cues and prompt the Explainer to adjust their language or invite the Listener to share. The Mediator uses phrases like, "I noticed you seemed puzzled; perhaps we could rephrase that?" or "Would you like to share your thoughts on reducing plastic waste?" (Pragmatic: Asking about impact, discussing ideas. Sociolinguistic: Polite phrasing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides sentence stems: "If we [plant more trees], we will [improve air quality]," "If people [recycled more], pollution [would reduce faster]."</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Explaining and Adapting (Pair Interaction/Peer Check)</b> (7 min)
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes a turn explaining a specific section of their partially completed "Forest Health Infographic" to their partner for 1-2 minutes. The listener provides non-verbal feedback.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The speaker must actively interpret cues and adapt their explanation, using simpler language or drawings if needed (e.g., "Deforestation means cutting down trees, which harms animals' habitats."). They should also incorporate comparatives (e.g., "Our forest health is significantly healthier now compared to last decade.") and conditional sentences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partners give feedback on how well the speaker interpreted their cues and adapted their explanation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task: Students present and mediate their "Forest Health Infographic" using target language.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will form small groups (3-4 students). Each student will take turns presenting and explaining their "Forest Health Infographic" to their group members. During the presentation, the presenter must actively observe their group for non-verbal cues (e.g., confusion, interest, disagreement). When a cue is observed, the presenter must pause, interpret the cue, and actively mediate understanding by:
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asking clarifying questions (e.g., "I noticed you seemed interested; would you like to share your thoughts on recycling?").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Simplifying complex terms with alternative wording or drawings/gestures (e.g., "Deforestation, meaning the cutting down of trees, significantly affects our climate.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inviting contributions or questions from group members (e.g., "How has deforestation affected our area?").</li>
            </ol>
            The group members, in turn, must provide genuine non-verbal feedback and be ready to contribute. Students should use comparatives from their infographics (e.g., "Biodiversity in protected areas is dramatically greater than in logged regions.") and conditionals (e.g., "If we continue unsustainable logging, our forest health will decline significantly."). (10-12 min)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small group collaborative presentation and facilitated discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme:</b> "How Our Ecology Is Changing" and Scenario: "Our Changing Forests."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in presenting and explaining the infographic, active demonstration of mediation skills (interpreting non-verbal cues, facilitating discussion, clarifying information), and accurate use of target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of mediation skills during the infographic presentation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment. The teacher uses a checklist focused on the learning outcomes, observing each student's mediation skills during their infographic explanation. Group members use a simple peer assessment form to rate how well their peers mediated understanding.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Forest Health Infographic" in small groups, actively interpreting non-verbal cues, facilitating discussion, and clarifying information. The focus is on *how* they present and mediate, not just the content of the infographic.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpretation of Non-Verbal Cues:</b> Accurately identifies and responds to at least two different non-verbal cues (e.g., confusion, interest).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitation of Discussion:</b> Successfully prompts classmates to contribute ideas or ask questions about conservation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Adaptation:</b> Simplifies complex language or uses visuals/gestures when needed to ensure understanding (e.g., explains 'biodiversity' simply or uses a drawing for 'deforestation').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Consistently uses present perfect, conditionals, and comparatives appropriately (e.g., "If we protect marine life, our rivers will be healthier," "Air quality is poorer in urban ecosystems compared to rural ones.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation & Fluency:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of /aɪ/ words and word stress, smooth delivery.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic Competence:</b> Expresses environmental concerns politely and uses community-focused language (e.g., "Together, we can reduce plastic waste.").</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I did well today to help my classmates understand my infographic was..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next time I explain a complex topic, I will try to improve my ability to [interpret non-verbal cues/facilitate discussion/clarify language] by..."</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and future planning.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when trying to interpret your classmates' non-verbal cues, and how did you try to overcome them?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using comparatives and conditionals in your infographic explanation help to clarify the impact of climate change or conservation efforts?"</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating students' mediation skills during the infographic explanations? (e.g., peer assessment, specific sentence stems for mediation).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with interpreting non-verbal cues or adapting their language? (e.g., more direct modeling, a visual aid for common non-verbal cues).</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "How Our Ecology Is Changing" theme. The mediation skills practiced, particularly in explaining complex environmental data and facilitating discussions, will be vital for future academic and professional contexts where students need to effectively communicate information and engage audiences. These skills will be transferable to new themes and projects, emphasizing critical thinking and collaborative problem-solving for sustainable development.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
    </tr>
</table>